      "| Cause | What's happening | The fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Trapped humidity, poor airflow | A sealed space with no exchange lets ambient moisture build up | Moisture absorber + occasional cracked-door airflow |\n| Damp or lightly worn laundry stored inside | Moisture and body oils keep breaking down in a closed space | Only store fully clean, fully dry items |\n| Old shoes | Sweat-soaked linings humidify the whole closet from the floor up | Air shoes out before storing; keep the rack ventilated |\n| Closet rarely opened | Humidity and odor concentrate with no exchange | Open the door regularly, even briefly |\n| Overpacked closet | No room for air to move between items | Leave breathing room; don't pack floor to ceiling |\n| Mildew on walls/baseboards | Active mold growth from a real moisture source | Treat the mildew and fix the underlying moisture — not a fragrance issue |",
      "Once you've solved for airflow and moisture, a musty basement can follow the same order of operations one level down — our guide on [why basements smell musty even when they're dry](/blog/why-does-my-basement-smell-musty) walks through the larger-space version of this same problem.",
      "## Frequently asked questions",
      "### Why does my closet smell musty even though it's clean?",
      "Because \"musty\" usually isn't about cleanliness — it's about trapped, stagnant, slightly humid air with nowhere to go. A closet is a small sealed box with little to no airflow, so even a spotless one can hold onto moisture from ambient humidity, lightly worn clothes, or shoes, and that trapped dampness is what produces the smell. Vacuuming and wiping shelves doesn't address the underlying airflow and moisture issue.",
      "### Will an air freshener or scented sachet fix a musty closet?",
      "Only temporarily, and only if there's no real moisture problem underneath. A spray or sachet adds fragrance on top of the existing air; it doesn't remove humidity or improve airflow, so the musty note typically returns within a day or two, now mixed with whatever you sprayed. Fix the moisture and airflow first with an absorber and a bit of ventilation, then add scent as the finishing layer — that's the order that actually lasts.",
      "### How do I know if it's just mustiness or actual mold?",
      "Ordinary mustiness smells flat, stale, and slightly damp, and it clears up within a couple of weeks of using a moisture absorber and improving airflow. Mold or mildew smells sharper or earthier, often shows up as dark or fuzzy spots on drywall, baseboards, or the closet's back wall, and doesn't improve with airflow alone. If you see visible growth or the smell persists after you've addressed humidity, treat it as a mold issue — clean it properly and, if it's widespread or recurring, get the underlying moisture source diagnosed rather than masking it.",
